course: dictionary
lesson: 8754
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary lists them with numbers, and the context tells you which one fits. Settlement is a good example. Read the words around it and ask: what is this sentence about?
explain: «Escaped enslaved people formed free communities called maroon settlements in Jamaica.» Here settlements are places where people live and build homes, like a village. The word «communities» is the clue, because communities are groups of people living together.
explain: «The two sides reached a settlement.» This is different. Here a settlement is an agreement that ends an argument. Try the village meaning in the sentence: "The two sides reached a village" does not make sense, so this must be the agreement meaning.
fact: Settlement means a place where people have come to live and built homes, such as a village or small town.
fact: Settlement can also mean an agreement that ends an argument or a legal case, as in «reach a settlement».
fact: «In settlement of a bill» means paying money that is owed.
fact: Settlement can also mean the slow sinking of a building or the ground under it.
exercise: «Odesa was founded in 1794 on the site of an older Ottoman settlement.» In this sentence, what is a settlement? | Money that is owed | An agreement that ends an argument | *A place where people lived and built homes | Ground that is sinking
exercise: «The two sides reached a settlement.» What does settlement mean here? | *An agreement that ends an argument | The sinking of a building | Paying money that is owed | A village by the river
exercise: Why were early settlements often built near rivers? | *To get water, food and transport | To hide from every visitor | To make the ground sink | To be far away from the sea
test: «About a billion people live in informal settlements, often called slums.» What does settlements mean here? | Paying money that is owed | Agreements that end arguments | *Places where people live, often without proper pipes and roads | The slow sinking of buildings
test: «Settlement of a bill» means what? | An argument between two people | Ground that slowly goes down | *Paying money that is owed | The act of settling a new land
exam: «English spread from one small island to more than 50 countries mainly through trade, settlement and empire.» In this sentence, what does settlement most likely mean? | An agreement that ended a quarrel | *The act of settling in a new land | Paying money that was owed | Slow sinking of the ground
